//
// `"{t}"` on a whole tuple is refused, and that refusal is a DECIDED non-goal with no cell.
//
// `formatting.md` `(F-Render)` gives a row to every scalar, to `vector` and to `struct`, and
// none to a tuple.  [TUPLES.md § Non-goals](../../doc/claude/TUPLES.md) is where the decision
// lives — whole-tuple formatting is a compile error, and the cure is a named struct or
// element-by-element access.  `(T-Absent)` gives the reason one level down: *"a tuple has no
// faithful document form anyway"*.
//
// Nothing pinned it.  A declared non-goal with no guard is the shape that rots quietest: it can
// become SUPPORTED by an unrelated change and nobody learns, or its message can drift into
// something that sends a reader looking for a misspelling — which is exactly what
// *"Unknown field vector.remove"* did for a nullable collection one issue over (loft#1453).
// The rules doc now states the absence and the reason; this is the cell that holds it.
//
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   everything.  This file is ONE cell on purpose: the refusal is a
//                        compile-time error, so any test function beside it never runs — the
//                        phase masking recorded in TESTING.md § How a guard reads green.  The
//                        renderable parts of a tuple are the sibling file
//                        `a-tuple-renders-through-its-members.loft`, and they are the half that
//                        stops a cure satisfying this by dropping tuples from the formatter
//                        entirely.
//
// @falsified-at: none — this pins a REFUSAL that has never been anything else, so there is no
//   build on which it fails; `make falsify` compares two binaries and both refuse.  What it
//   guards against is a FUTURE change making the refusal disappear or reword, which is the
//   direction a `none` opt-out is for.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot format type (integer, integer)
fn whole_tuple_is_refused() -> text {
  t = (1, 2);
  "{t}"
}
